1. `Creating Directories`: we will use `mkdir` + `name` this name will the name of the 
    directory which we curated and as this is relative path this directory will be get 
    created in our current working directory and `mkdir` is make directory. 
    
    
2. `Creating subdirectories in a directory`: `mkdir` is not just limited to create a directory
    only `mkdir -p ../Project/data ../Project/results` by this combining `-p` allows this we gone
    one directory previous using `..` and then we created `data` and `results` sub directories in
    `Project` directory. we can even achieve this creating directory or subdirectory task by file
    explorer directly but some cases using terminal tends to be more efficient.
    
    
3. `Good names for files or directories`: some tips for naming for files and directories while curating
    them using terminal.
    
		1. `Avoid spaces and use either - or _`: as command prompt consider spaces as separation and would
		    create multiple directories or files of that name instead of giving one instead of use `- or _` as
		    `north_pacific_gyre` or `north-pacific-gyre` both are correct but avoid `north pacific gyre`.
		2. `Dont begin name with -` : as command treat `-` as taking options.
		3. `Stick with lower case letters, numbers , full stop , - , _`: try to avoid special characters as 
		    they have diffeernt meanings when use in terminal and even if you wanted to void these rules 
	        write the name in single quotes that will treated as it is as you want and won't create any problem.
	        It is often recommended to use lower case letters in file names because windows and macos terminals
	        are often case insensitive and often treate thesis and Thesis as same. 
	        
	        
4. `creating text file using terminal`: `nano draft.txt` using this `nano` editor will open with a file `draft`
    as `txt` format make sure this `nano` editor is most easy to learn and use and it only supports basic work
    only like plain texts , no images , no tables etc supported. after typing `nano draft.txt` this will open 
    text terminal and then type what you wanted to in this file and to keep the name default we typed with command
    press `ctrl + o` and then press `enter` and then `ctrl + x` now our `draft.txt` is created with the data we gave
    input , we can even use default text editor to save the file at what location we wanted usually we use this to 
    create a file at current working directory.`nano` doesn't leave any output on the terminal but you can see file 
    created in the directory by `ls`. 
    
    
5. `creating empty file using terminal and when this require`: `touch new_file.txt` now our current directory will 
    have this file with 0 byte that means empty file this requires us when some programs require a empty file to 
    fill output get from that particular program.And size of empty file will be of 0 bytes.
    
    
6. `something.something`: any file is written in this format usually , second part after dot is extension and this 
    helps to distinguish files like `.txt` for text files , `.pdf` for pdf and so on. 
    
    
7. `moving files and directories`: `mv thesis/drafts.txt thesis/quotes.txt` this will rename our `drafts.txt` to 
    `quotes.txt` why because when both address are same then `mv` will work as renaming. As we are saying `mv`(moving)
    a file from a address to same address just different name then this will act as renaming. now moving any file 
    `mv thesis/quotes.txt .` we gave second argument as final location that is our current directory and this will move 
    our file. We can even pair to move files like `mv count.txt check.txt ~/Desktop` then these two files will move to 
    desktop in this we can pair multiple files from our current directory to move.make sure first either you are copying 
    any file or directory relative then your current directory must has both destinations which file you are copying and
    to which directory , now copying file `cp quotes.txt thesis/quotes.txt` and to copy directory `cp -r alkanes anima-counts/alkanes`
    and we included this r because this will copy all contents of the directory which we copied.making copy of a whole 
    directory for backup in current working directory use `cp -r animal-counts animal-counts-backup`. for both cp and 
    mv if you provide same destination and a different name with same extension this will act as renaming wherease different
    location will act as copying or moving . 
    
    
8. `removing files and directories`: `rm files.txt` this will remove `files.txt` if this present in our current working 
    directory. by deleting from terminal we deleted for forever we can use some tools to recover but there is no guarantee.
    but `rm -i files.txt` will ask to if we wanted to delete or not type `Y` for yes and `N` for no. Deleting directory
    use `rm -r -data` this will delete recurssively all files and content from `data` directory and even whole 
    directory itself `rm -i -r -data` this will confirm whether we wanted to delete `data` then `Y` for yes and 
    `N` for no and then ask for each file and content present in that directory and we have to confirm for each 
    content manually. Because we are doing permanant deletion we will get a change to view what we are deleting by that `-i`.
    
    
9. `working with multiple directories or files`: whether we wanted to delete any file , directory individually or multiple
    together just list name and if we are copying and moving then when there will be more than two arguments then last one
    will be treated as destination and for deletion we can simply link them one by one . but make sure last argument should
    be location otherwise you will hit error.
    
    
10. `wild card characters`: 
		1. `*`: when this combined with any extension like `*.txt` then this will list all files having that extension in
	    our current working directory and when used as `pa*` then this will list all files having `pa` as prefix we can use
	    for any number of valid letters as prefix. and even `*check.txt` will present this `check.txt` and even all other 
	    files having that `check` as end  of name. 
		2. `?`: this is used as a ignorant means `??check.txt` and this will show all files having `check.txt` after ignoring 
		first 2 characters of name.
		3. `Note`: wildcards alone won't work you have to combine them with `ls` command to use them and if there is no match
		found then would return error otherwise matched contents will flash. 
